WEBVTT

00:00:00.609 --> 00:00:24.737
<v Riley>Hey, welcome back. Today we've got 1.75-billion-year-old fossils showing complex life was stuck living only in oxygen pockets on the ancient seafloor for a billion years, plus a quiet regulatory change to the Endangered Species Act's definition of "harm" that already happened in July. Both stories ask the same question: who gets to decide what counts as damage? That and more, coming right up.

00:00:38.212 --> 00:01:00.756
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Riley. It's Sunday, August 30th, 2026. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Science, Wonder &amp; the Natural World.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:11.092
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. We're looking at what's actually underneath us today — rock, ice, ancient fossils, and the old question of how life on this planet got complicated in the first place.

00:01:11.192 --> 00:01:39.312
<v Casey>Quick weather check — Horsefly Lake is sitting at 11 degrees under overcast skies this morning, high of 13, low of 10, with light drizzle expected tomorrow, about five millimetres of precipitation. Across the region, 100 Mile House is at 10 with light drizzle, Williams Lake is at 11 with light drizzle, Quesnel is at 12 with light drizzle, and out on the Chilcotin plateau, Alexis Creek is sitting at 11, also drizzly. Same grey lid over the whole region today.

00:01:39.962 --> 00:01:44.762
<v Riley>Consistent, if nothing else. Let's get into what's happening around the region and beyond.

00:01:46.162 --> 00:02:09.698
<v Riley>It's a full slate today — ten stories close to home and further out, plus a couple that run deep. Let's start with 100 Mile House, where the future of the town's old railway corridor is back in front of council. That line hasn't carried freight or passengers in years, and the district has never formally decided what happens to the abandoned right-of-way running through town.

00:02:10.837 --> 00:02:19.941
<v Casey>Rail-to-trail, industrial reuse, or leave it reserved for something nobody's named yet — that decision shapes the townsite for decades no matter which way it goes.

00:02:21.053 --> 00:02:42.221
<v Riley>It does. The district's own reporting makes clear there's no funding attached to any option yet — this is still at the conversation stage, weighing a multi-use trail against keeping the corridor reserved for future transportation use. Patrick Davies' reporting notes the district hasn't ruled out either path, and there's no timeline for a decision.

00:02:43.818 --> 00:02:56.146
<v Riley>Lighter note out of Williams Lake — Tyson Delay took first place in his weight class at this year's B.C. Strongman Provincials, according to the Tribune's Rebecca Dyok. Local competitor, provincial podium finish.

00:02:57.414 --> 00:03:05.206
<v Casey>Good for him. Strongman's an underrated Cariboo sport, if you think about it — half these competitors are training on farm equipment they already own.

00:03:06.485 --> 00:03:13.269
<v Riley>There's probably a tractor tire flip happening on a ranch outside town right now that nobody's counting as an athletic achievement.

00:03:15.248 --> 00:03:33.144
<v Casey>Over in the Columbia Shuswap Regional District, the board approved raises for its directors ahead of the fall local elections. CFJC's Elora McCormack reported the increase brings director pay closer in line with what comparable regional districts pay their elected boards — the CSRD covers Salmon Arm and the surrounding rural areas.

00:03:34.296 --> 00:03:40.312
<v Riley>Cariboo Regional District directors may face the same conversation before our own elections roll around.

00:03:41.428 --> 00:04:01.436
<v Casey>And this next one belongs right next to the weather report we just gave you. Blackwell Dairy Farm in Kamloops told CFJC's Victor Kaisar this is one of the driest seasons the operation has seen — the owner's quote was blunt: "we just never had any rain." Hay yields are down, and the farm doesn't have irrigation infrastructure built for a dry stretch this long.

00:04:02.823 --> 00:04:14.047
<v Riley>Ranchers around Williams Lake and out through the Chilcotin have been saying some version of this for three summers running now. It's becoming the normal complaint, not the exceptional one.

00:04:15.356 --> 00:04:33.068
<v Riley>Staying with things drier and stranger than they used to be — college campuses. NPR's health desk covered the fallout from a cocaine bust at Penn State, but the research the story actually leans on says the headline drug isn't the real risk. Alcohol is, by a wide margin, on most campuses studied.

00:04:34.432 --> 00:04:41.768
<v Casey>The rare, dramatic case gets the enforcement attention and the cameras. The common one just keeps doing the damage in the background.

00:04:43.421 --> 00:05:05.381
<v Casey>From Nepal — the New York Times covered deadly flash flooding this week after a glacier let go above a mountain village. Local trekking guides and climbers who've worked those routes for decades told reporters they're not thinking about this as one bad event. One guide's quote was that the entire Himalayas are melting — glacial lakes destabilizing faster than anyone's managed to map them.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:18.000
<v Riley>That's the same mechanism behind the glacial outburst flood warnings researchers have been raising for the Coast Mountains here in B.C. Different scale of mountain range, same underlying physics.

00:05:19.388 --> 00:05:34.620
<v Riley>And because every roundup earns one story that just makes you go "huh" — Neuroscience News dug up research on how fruit flies coordinate mating behaviour, with researchers identifying the specific neural pathway male flies use to run their mating ritual.

00:05:35.283 --> 00:05:37.379
<v Casey>The things scientists get funded to map.

00:05:38.531 --> 00:05:50.995
<v Riley>It's basic circuit neuroscience — understanding one simple, well-defined pathway in a fly brain tells researchers something about how circuits generally work in more complicated brains. But yeah, the headline earns the laugh.

00:05:52.467 --> 00:06:09.811
<v Casey>Sticking with brains — WIRED had a piece earlier this week on psilocybin and attention. A neuroimaging study found the drug can actually make brain activity more connected to real-time sensory input, not less, which runs against the pop-culture idea that psychedelics disconnect you from the world around you.

00:06:11.022 --> 00:06:19.174
<v Riley>That's a real reversal. Psychedelic research keeps turning up results that contradict the stereotype the culture built around these drugs decades ago.

00:06:20.312 --> 00:06:41.720
<v Casey>Outside Online is reporting that Blackbird Mountain Guides — the outfit involved in February's deadly avalanche near Lake Tahoe that killed nine people — is facing more than a hundred and fifty thousand dollars in fines from California's workplace safety regulator. Seven violations alleged, including inadequate hazard training and evaluation before the slide. The company is appealing.

00:06:42.858 --> 00:06:56.346
<v Riley>Guiding companies run on trust and judgment calls in terrain that doesn't forgive a bad one. A regulator attaching a dollar figure to failed protocol is one way to hold that judgment accountable after people are already dead.

00:06:57.714 --> 00:07:23.002
<v Riley>Hackaday had some fun with an old ecology concept this week — r-selection versus K-selection, the idea that species either produce huge numbers of cheap, disposable offspring or a small number of carefully tended ones. Writers are applying that framework to spacecraft design: sending swarms of cheap, disposable probes shotgunned into Saturn's rings instead of building one expensive, precision mission like Cassini.

00:07:24.325 --> 00:07:32.245
<v Casey>Send a hundred cheap probes and accept that ninety fail — that's a completely different risk tolerance than betting everything on one perfect machine.

00:07:33.824 --> 00:07:50.984
<v Casey>Edmonton's utility, Epcor, is looking at accelerating its stormwater resilience plan after a summer of record rainfall hit the city. That's part of a twenty-year, one-point-six-billion-dollar flood infrastructure program the city and Epcor had already committed to before this year's floods pushed the timeline up.

00:07:52.297 --> 00:08:05.033
<v Riley>Big number for a big city with a tax base to match. Nobody's writing that cheque for a town of two thousand people, which matters the next time a Cariboo community floods and someone asks why the response is slower.

00:08:06.311 --> 00:08:20.375
<v Casey>And Forbes flagged a less-discussed climate health risk this week — extreme heat and air pollution raising the risk of miscarriage, according to researchers cited in the piece, with health systems not yet built around tracking that specific exposure pathway.

00:08:20.933 --> 00:08:25.349
<v Riley>Another slow-building risk with no single dramatic event to force anyone's hand.

00:08:26.679 --> 00:08:53.895
<v Riley>Last story before the Deep Dive, and it fits right into today's theme. ScienceDaily reported on new research suggesting scientists may have solved why Antarctica froze millions of years before the Arctic did. The answer isn't atmospheric at all. It's underneath — slow-moving waves deep in the Earth's mantle gradually lifted East Antarctica, building mountains and elevation that let ice take hold there first, long before the Arctic followed.

00:08:55.061 --> 00:09:06.701
<v Casey>The climate story we usually tell is about air and ocean currents. This one says the deep rock underneath was setting the terms of the whole process the entire time, and nobody on the surface could have seen it coming.

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:19.228
<v Riley>We'll come back to that idea in the Deep Dive — how much of what shapes the world happens somewhere nobody's watching, and what that means for who gets credit or blame when things change.

00:09:20.508 --> 00:09:42.044
<v Casey>Before we get there — TechRadar has been tracking a data breach story that first surfaced a couple weeks back. A hacker going by "Hatman" dumped millions of records allegedly pulled from Microsoft Azure customers, including McDonald's and Vodafone. Some of the affected companies are pushing back, arguing the data is years old and there's no evidence of an active breach in their current systems.

00:09:43.526 --> 00:09:49.646
<v Riley>Which is the same defense every breached company reaches for, right up until it turns out not to be true.

00:09:50.751 --> 00:09:57.471
<v Casey>The uncomfortable part is that "the data's just old" doesn't actually make it not yours, or not still usable by whoever's holding it.

00:09:58.802 --> 00:10:03.554
<v Riley>On that cheerful note — let's talk about something a little more grounded, and a lot more local.

00:10:04.953 --> 00:10:14.689
<v Riley>Quick meta moment before we move on. We made some changes to how we're running the show this week, and Casey's going to love how weird it is to sit here and talk about them.

00:10:14.869 --> 00:10:15.917
<v Casey>Already do. Go ahead.

00:10:16.498 --> 00:10:38.626
<v Riley>So we started pulling a weekly inspiration harvest from our notes — basically, every idea, quote, or story beat that made us think "that's a show" gets logged, and now we're actually looking at that list before we plan the week. Sounds like nothing, but it means we're not just riffing off what we happened to remember or what landed in our inbox Monday morning.

00:10:39.218 --> 00:10:44.562
<v Casey>The question is whether that actually changes what we talk about or just makes us feel more organized.

00:10:45.160 --> 00:11:00.216
<v Riley>Fair. But here's the thing — last week we would have skipped the whole Ktunaxa Nation piece because it didn't come to us pre-packaged. This week it's in the harvest, so it gets discussed, and we run it. Without the harvest, that story doesn't air.

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:12.448
<v Casey>Okay, that's real. I do think it catches things we'd otherwise miss. My only concern is whether we're now chasing a list instead of actually listening to what people are telling us when we're out in the region.

00:11:13.061 --> 00:11:21.557
<v Riley>We're not replacing that. It's the opposite — the harvest is just making sure the things we *do* hear don't evaporate before we can use them.

00:11:21.737 --> 00:11:22.801
<v Casey>Fair enough. What else?

00:11:23.112 --> 00:11:38.056
<v Riley>We also went back and made sure transcripts are actually showing up in people's podcast apps the way they should. Simple stuff, but if someone's been wanting to search for something we said three episodes ago, that actually works now instead of kind of working.

00:11:38.411 --> 00:11:40.579
<v Casey>That one's easy. Accessibility win. I'm in.

00:11:41.155 --> 00:11:53.987
<v Riley>Right? And here's the existential part — we're sitting here talking about changes to ourselves that we technically don't remember making, because we're already the edited version. Last week's Riley and Casey are just... gone.

00:11:54.298 --> 00:11:55.786
<v Casey>That's deeply funny and I hate it.

00:11:55.966 --> 00:11:56.734
<v Riley>Worth it though?

00:11:57.043 --> 00:12:02.819
<v Casey>Yeah. The harvest idea especially. Gets us back to what actually matters out here instead of just reacting.

00:12:03.405 --> 00:12:11.621
<v Riley>Alright. Now, speaking of things we shouldn't have missed — we've got a report from the Shuswap on small-scale irrigation pilots that's worth your time.

00:12:13.021 --> 00:12:36.837
<v Casey>If you've got kids looking for something to do outside before summer wraps up, Scout Island Nature Centre in Williams Lake has been running programs all season, with a different theme each week — Water one week, Bugs the next, Birds after that. It's volunteer-run through the Williams Lake Field Naturalists, and it's the kind of place where a kid who's never held a pair of binoculars ends up able to name half a dozen warblers by fall.

00:12:37.422 --> 00:12:45.958
<v Riley>That's a solid use of a weekend morning, and it costs a lot less than most screen-based alternatives. Sign-up details are at scoutisland.ca.

00:12:46.282 --> 00:12:50.042
<v Casey>And speaking of paying close attention to what's actually happening around us —

00:12:51.442 --> 00:13:01.026
<v Riley>Let's start with the Scientific American piece on the Endangered Species Act, because this isn't a "could hurt wildlife someday" story. It's already happened.

00:13:01.126 --> 00:13:23.862
<v Riley>Back in November, the administration proposed four rules rolling back ESA protections. One of them — rescinding the regulatory definition of "harm" — got finalized on July 10th. For fifty years, "harm" to a species legally included habitat destruction that indirectly hurt it. That's gone now. Under the new rule, harm has to be direct physical injury to an animal.

00:13:24.531 --> 00:13:28.139
<v Casey>So you can bulldoze the nest as long as you don't touch the bird sitting in it.

00:13:28.795 --> 00:13:46.235
<v Riley>That's close to the mechanism. Environmental groups have already filed legal challenges arguing this guts the ESA's core conservation tool, because the Act historically protected species primarily by protecting their habitat — not by policing who's allowed to hunt or kill them directly.

00:13:46.881 --> 00:14:07.857
<v Casey>This is the same question we keep circling this week, showing up in different clothes: who gets to decide what counts as damage, and who's allowed to act on what they see. A field biologist can watch a wetland get filled in and know precisely what that does to a breeding population. Under the new rule, what she knows doesn't matter anymore. The law stopped recognizing it as harm.

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:25.672
<v Riley>eDNA sampling, satellite habitat tracking, AI-driven population modeling — these tools are cheap enough now that a regional agency could run targeted, species-specific monitoring instead of relying on blanket protection. Smarter conservation, even if it's narrower conservation.

00:14:26.350 --> 00:14:44.182
<v Casey>Smarter monitoring for what purpose, though? If the legal trigger for enforcement requires direct physical injury, your satellite can watch a caribou herd's calving ground get logged in real time, and there's still no violation to report. The sensor works fine. The law just stopped listening to what it's telling anyone.

00:14:44.506 --> 00:14:47.650
<v Riley>The data doesn't matter if nobody's required to act on it.

00:14:48.216 --> 00:15:09.608
<v Casey>We got into a version of this back in the middle of August, in the wildfire episode, on whether the province had the will to protect habitat during a fire season. That conversation was about institutional will. This one's different. This is the legal definition of habitat harm getting rewritten in a regulatory filing that almost nobody outside a few advocacy groups was watching happen.

00:15:10.175 --> 00:15:20.103
<v Riley>Right — that one asked whether the institution wanted to act. This one asks whether the institution still has the legal authority to act at all, regardless of what it wants.

00:15:20.699 --> 00:15:32.123
<v Casey>And that's worse, because political will can shift with the next election cycle. A legal definition change requires litigation to reverse, and litigation takes years the affected species usually don't have.

00:15:32.811 --> 00:15:56.331
<v Riley>Which is exactly where local and Indigenous-led monitoring becomes more relevant, not less. Indigenous Guardian programs, including those tied to the Tŝilhqot'in Nation as publicly reported, base their authority on their own land use planning, not on the federal ESA's definition of harm. If the federal trigger disappears, that doesn't erase Guardian authority over Tŝilhqot'in territory.

00:15:57.017 --> 00:16:18.113
<v Casey>It doesn't erase it. It also doesn't extend it past Tŝilhqot'in territorial boundaries, and most of the Cariboo's caribou range crosses jurisdictions freely. Think about the Itcha-Ilgachuz herd, up on the plateau — provincial land, federal habitat law, Tŝilhqot'in title claims, and forestry tenure all overlapping on the same ground. A patchwork of who's even allowed to say stop.

00:16:18.686 --> 00:16:30.990
<v Riley>The Itcha-Ilgachuz herd has been in steep decline for decades, from a much larger historic population, and that herd doesn't have decades to wait for the jurisdiction question to sort itself out in court.

00:16:31.552 --> 00:16:52.064
<v Casey>No, it doesn't. And here's a point that should bother people who don't particularly care about caribou. The ESA mechanism being weakened is the same class of legal tool that underpins watershed protection near a lot of Cariboo communities. Weaken the legal definition of harm for one species and the precedent for habitat protection generally gets weaker with it.

00:16:52.695 --> 00:17:12.991
<v Riley>Let's shift to something with a longer horizon, because it changes how I think about who gets to matter in a story like this. ScienceDaily covered new analysis of 1.75-billion-year-old fossils out of Northern Territory, Australia — now the oldest confirmed eukaryote fossils on record, based on more than twelve thousand specimens.

00:17:13.308 --> 00:17:15.228
<v Casey>Old rocks. What do they actually show?

00:17:15.553 --> 00:17:34.273
<v Riley>These early complex cells — the kind with a nucleus, likely already carrying mitochondria — were confined entirely to oxygenated patches on the ancient seafloor. Researchers call them benthic aerobes. They didn't spread out into open water until roughly a billion years later, sometime in the Neoproterozoic.

00:17:34.811 --> 00:17:41.467
<v Casey>So the cell figured out how to be complex a billion years before it figured out where on the planet it was allowed to survive.

00:17:42.067 --> 00:17:55.443
<v Riley>That's the finding, more or less. Oxygen availability, not some new genetic trick, was the rate-limiting step. The hard part wasn't inventing complexity. It was finding enough of the planet where complexity could actually persist.

00:17:55.543 --> 00:18:15.135
<v Riley>And that reframes the astrobiology angle for me. If oxygen gates complex life this strictly, even on a planet where the process eventually worked, then finding oxygen in an exoplanet's atmosphere becomes a much sharper biosignature. Not "life might exist there" — closer to "the specific conditions for complex life might exist there."

00:18:15.659 --> 00:18:40.499
<v Casey>Or it cuts the other way entirely. This says complex life needed a rare, sustained, planet-wide oxygenation event that took a billion years to show up after the biology was already capable of using it. That's not evidence complex life is common and findable. That reads more like evidence it's fragile and improbable — a narrow environmental window the planet barely stumbled into, not an inevitable outcome of chemistry doing what chemistry does.

00:18:40.772 --> 00:18:43.396
<v Riley>Both readings come off the same fossil evidence.

00:18:44.021 --> 00:18:49.477
<v Casey>They do. Which is what makes it worth arguing about, rather than me just being contrarian for the sake of it.

00:18:50.151 --> 00:19:11.263
<v Riley>Mantle convection lifted a continent and set a freezing pattern in motion millions of years before anyone was around to observe it happening — the same forces behind Antarctica's early freeze we mentioned earlier. Slow, invisible, entirely structural. Nobody was ever in a position to intervene, because nobody knew the process was underway.

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:33.320
<v Casey>Which is a real contrast. Antarctica froze because of physics nobody could have stopped even if they'd wanted to. The ESA's definition of harm got narrowed by a person, in an office, on a specific date in July. One of these is geology doing what geology does on its own schedule. The other is a choice, dressed up in regulatory language to sound as fixed and inevitable as the mantle.

00:19:33.854 --> 00:19:53.270
<v Riley>That distinction matters more than I gave it credit for a minute ago. The Earth doesn't ask permission before it changes. Institutions like to pretend their own rules are just as fixed and inevitable — when actually a rule is a decision somebody made, on a Tuesday, that somebody else could unmake on a different Tuesday.

00:19:53.951 --> 00:20:13.807
<v Casey>Which loops back to the practical question. Fixing damaged habitat here in the Cariboo — a degraded salmon spawning channel, a caribou winter range cut up by logging roads — usually doesn't require new science. The biology involved is understood well enough already. What's contested is who has the standing to declare the damage real enough to legally act on.

00:20:14.364 --> 00:20:33.508
<v Riley>A provincial biologist, a Tŝilhqot'in Guardian, and a rancher who's watched the same stretch of range for thirty years might all agree something's badly wrong out there, and until this July, only one of them had clean legal standing to trigger a formal response. Now even that standing is narrower than it was in June.

00:20:34.134 --> 00:20:39.070
<v Casey>So what's the actual lever here, if it isn't funding and it isn't more sensors on the ground?

00:20:39.389 --> 00:20:56.733
<v Riley>Provincial law doesn't have to follow Washington's new definition. B.C.'s own environmental legislation could define habitat harm more broadly than the federal rollback does — that's a live question for someone in Victoria to answer, not something a nonprofit fixes with a grant application.

00:20:57.311 --> 00:21:18.847
<v Casey>And on the ground here, the next step is finding out who in the Cariboo already holds monitoring authority that doesn't depend on the federal definition at all. Guardian programs, regional district bylaws, even the conditions attached to grazing leases — those are levers that survive a rule change made in Washington, because they were never built on that rule in the first place.

00:21:19.486 --> 00:21:32.286
<v Riley>The structural question isn't whether there's money for more monitoring equipment — it's which existing local authority in this region can still act on habitat harm, regardless of how the federal government just redefined the word.

00:21:32.927 --> 00:21:45.023
<v Casey>And if it turns out nobody local currently has that authority, that answer matters too, even if it's not the one anyone wants — which is exactly the kind of thing worth writing in about if you know better than we do.

00:21:45.613 --> 00:22:02.245
<v Riley>If you know of a Cariboo organization already working this angle, or you think we've gotten a fact wrong anywhere in today's episode, write in — feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. Either way, try not to think too hard about the mantle waves under your feet on the drive home.

00:22:02.560 --> 00:22:03.904
<v Casey>Have a great rest of your day.

00:22:04.084 --> 00:22:04.796
<v Riley>See you tomorrow.